          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A biopsy slide shows short, branching muscle fibres with a dark transverse line at points where neighbouring cells meet, and a single oval nucleus placed centrally in each fibre. Which muscle type is this?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Skeletal muscle</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Skeletal muscle fibres are long, parallel, and do not branch, and their nuclei are multiple and peripheral, not central — this does not match the description.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Cardiac muscle</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. Branching fibres with intercalated discs (the dark transverse lines where cells interdigitate) and a single central oval nucleus are the hallmark of cardiac muscle. The intercalated disc is the single most reliable diagnostic feature.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Smooth muscle</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Smooth muscle cells are spindle-shaped with a single cigar-shaped nucleus, but they show no striations and never branch or form intercalated discs.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Skeletal muscle undergoing regeneration</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Even regenerating skeletal fibres remain long and non-branching; they do not form intercalated discs, which are unique to cardiac muscle.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Remember: branching fibres + intercalated discs + a central oval nucleus = cardiac muscle. This is the most reliable diagnostic combination among the three muscle types."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Well done — the intercalated disc is the single feature that unambiguously identifies cardiac muscle on a slide."
                }
              ],
