          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>In skeletal muscle, a triad is formed by one transverse tubule flanked by two te</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>In skeletal muscle, a triad is formed by one transverse tubule flanked by two terminal cisternae of the sarcoplasmic reticulum. Where in the sarcomere is this triad located?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>At the Z-line</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>The Z-line marks the sarcomere boundary and is where titin anchors, but the triad in skeletal muscle sits at the A-I band junction, not the Z-line — that arrangement (one cisterna at the Z-line) is the cardiac dyad instead.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>At the junction between the A-band and the I-band</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. In skeletal muscle, each transverse tubule lies at the A-I band junction, flanked by two terminal cisternae, forming the triad that allows rapid, synchronised calcium release across the fibre.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>At the centre of the H-zone</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>The centre of the H-zone is where the M-line cross-links myosin filaments — this is a structural landmark, not the site of T-tubule invagination.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>At the M-line</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>The M-line cross-links myosin filaments at the centre of the sarcomere; the triad is located at the A-I band junction, not here.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "The triad forms at the A-I band junction: one transverse tubule flanked by two terminal cisternae of the sarcoplasmic reticulum. This positioning ensures the action potential triggers calcium release uniformly across the sarcomere."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Exactly right — the A-I band junction is where the T-tubule and its two flanking terminal cisternae form the triad, enabling near-simultaneous calcium release throughout the fibre."
                }
              ],
